{"timestamp": "2026-01-08T15:54:16.468836", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_architect_completed", "data": {"problem_analysis": "The core challenge lies in jointly optimizing INA switch placement and worker-to-aggregator assignment under strict hardware and network constraints. Key data structures include job-specific worker and PS mappings, switch tier classifications, precomputed shortest paths, and per-link bandwidth capacities. The solution must balance INA deployment budget, switch processing limits, and link bandwidth saturation while ensuring valid routing assignments. The critical insight is that placement and assignment are interdependent: poor placement creates hotspots, while poor assignment wastes INA capacity.", "strategy_overview": "The solution follows a constructive, single-pass pipeline: first, prioritize candidate switches for INA based on topological influence and job proximity; second, assign workers to aggregation points using a greedy, bottleneck-aware strategy; finally, assemble the solution using the precomputed components. The approach leverages existing data preprocessing and path-finding utilities to avoid redundant computation.", "function_count": 3, "functions": ["_decide_ina_placement_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators", "solve_with_heuristic"]}}
