{"timestamp": "2026-01-08T15:55:33.132169", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "**Observation:** The Core Layer link (Core(178) --> Aggr(172)) is at 100% utilization, indicating severe cross-Pod congestion. Meanwhile, multiple ToR switches (e.g., ToR(165) at 96.9%) are heavily utilized while Aggregation switches (Aggr(172), Aggr(174)) are underutilized (~32-36%), and PS nodes are idle despite active jobs. This reveals a critical misalignment between INA placement and traffic routing. **Insight:** The v1.0 heuristic assigns workers to INA switches based solely on shortest path length (i.e., minimal hop count), without considering the hierarchical cost of cross-Pod traffic or the ability of an INA switch to reduce congestion at the core. The algorithm fails to recognize that placing INA within a Pod (especially at ToR or Aggr level) can aggregate traffic locally and prevent cross-Pod forwarding, thereby alleviating core bottlenecks. The current score function weights all switch contributions equally by job size, ignoring the topological layer and the cost of traffic movement. **Directive:** Retain the centrality-based scoring for INA candidates, but modify the scoring to penalize switches that force traffic to traverse cross-Pod links (e.g., via a path cost function that increases with core-layer hops). Prioritize INA placement on switches that serve jobs with fragmented workloads and can perform local aggregation within a Pod. Additionally, during worker assignment, do not default to the PS unless no valid local INA switch exists; instead, explicitly evaluate whether routing via a local INA switch reduces overall cross-Pod traffic. Avoid rigid path-length-only assignment; instead, use a composite cost function that combines hop count, Pod affinity, and switch processing load.", "functions_to_update": ["_decide_ina_placement_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
