{"timestamp": "2026-01-08T15:58:51.936234", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "Observation: The algorithm selected an INA-enabled Aggr(172) switch with zero traffic (WASTED BUDGET), while ToR(165) was severely overloaded (90.1% utilization) and ToR(166) was saturated at 100%, indicating poor load balancing. This suggests that the placement heuristic prioritized topological centrality (e.g., cross-Pod traffic reduction) over actual load distribution, leading to hotspot formation. Furthermore, the PS for Job 2 (Server 6) is underutilized (46.8%) despite 17 workers, implying that aggregation was not effectively reducing the volume sent to PS, likely because the assigned INA switch (Aggr 172) was not receiving traffic. Insight: The current placement logic uses a static score based on job size and cross-Pod traffic, but ignores the dynamic load balancing implications of INA placement. The assignment logic assigns workers based on shortest path cost without considering the processing capacity of candidate switches, leading to overloading of specific ToR switches. Directive: Update the placement heuristic to incorporate switch processing capacity and load distribution potential; modify the assignment logic to include real-time capacity-aware routing and avoid assigning multiple workers to a single switch unless its capacity can be sustained. Avoid placing INA on switches that are not on high-traffic paths or that would not reduce PS load.", "functions_to_update": ["_decide_ina_placement_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
