{"timestamp": "2026-01-08T16:09:13.919752", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "**Observation:** A single ToR switch (ToR164) is severely overloaded (utilization > 100% in processing capacity), while other INA-enabled switches (ToR165, Aggr172) are completely idle despite being part of the budget. Additionally, multiple PS nodes are idle despite active jobs. **Insight:** The heuristic fails to balance load across INA candidates and ignores the physical reality of cross-Pod congestion and PS bottlenecking. The INA placement logic prioritizes topological centrality and path volume but does not simulate or penalize potential hotspots, leading to poor load distribution. Furthermore, worker assignment uses a greedy, hop-count-based cost function that favors same-ToR INA switches without considering whether the chosen INA switch is actually capable of handling the aggregate load, resulting in overloading a single switch. The assignment also lacks feedback from switch processing capacity constraints, creating a disconnect between placement and routing. **Directive:** Retain the topological scoring for INA placement but introduce dynamic load estimation based on expected aggregated traffic volume at each candidate switch. Modify the worker assignment to incorporate real-time load-aware routing, where each switch's current processing load (in Gbps) is used as a penalty in the cost function. Enforce a minimum load threshold for INA switches to prevent budget waste on idle nodes, and ensure that at least one INA switch per Pod with fragmented jobs is used to avoid PS bottlenecks.", "functions_to_update": ["_decide_ina_placement_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
