{"timestamp": "2026-01-08T16:10:40.118273", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "Observation: A single ToR switch (ToR164) is critically overloaded (100% utilization) while another INA-enabled switch (ToR165) is completely idle despite being budgeted and placed, indicating severe imbalance in workload distribution. This directly correlates to the high iteration makespan (34.90s) compared to the baseline (15.88s). Root Cause: The `_decide_ina_placement_candidates` function prioritizes switches based on a static score combining job size, pod fragmentation, and same-ToR bonus, but fails to account for actual switch processing capacity or spatial load balancing. The greedy selection of top-ranked switches without considering future assignment load leads to over-concentration in one pod. Additionally, the `_assign_workers_to_aggregators` function uses a path-cost heuristic that heavily favors same-ToR INA switches but does not enforce load-aware routing or detect overloading during assignment. As a result, the algorithm assigns many workers to ToR164 without checking its capacity, while ToR165 remains unused because it was not considered a high-scoring candidate under the current metric. Strategic Insight: Greedy, top-down placement based on static topology scores without feedback from assignment feasibility leads to hotspot formation. The assignment logic must be aware of resource load and avoid overloading any single switch, even if it appears topologically optimal. Actionable Directive: Modify the INA placement logic to incorporate a load-aware, capacity-constrained selection that avoids over-concentrating placements in any pod and ensures that placed switches are both topologically strategic and load-balancing. Update the worker assignment to include dynamic load estimation and rejection of over-capacity switches, even if they are in the placement set.", "functions_to_update": ["_decide_ina_placement_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
