{"timestamp": "2026-01-08T16:15:38.719905", "level": "INFO", "event": "heuristic_reflection_completed", "data": {"experience_lessons": "Observation: Despite allocating 5 INA switches (25% of total), only one switch (ToR167) saw non-trivial utilization (~6.9%), while three INA-enabled switches (ToR161, Aggr168, and others) were completely idle despite incurring budget cost. Simultaneously, the PS at Server(95) was saturated (100% utilization) due to 38 workers from Job 3 sending raw gradients directly, indicating INA failed to reduce volume. **Insight:** The heuristic prioritization in `_decide_ina_placement_candidates` was based on a composite score that rewarded same-Pod ToR switches and penalized cross-Pod traffic, but it did not account for actual traffic volume or load balancing across PSs. This led to selecting INA switches in low-traffic pods (e.g., ToR161) instead of high-traffic PSs (e.g., Server95). Additionally, the assignment logic in `_assign_workers_to_aggregators` restricted valid candidates to only same-Pod switches, excluding the PS’s own ToR switch (ToR164) for Job 3, which was the actual bottleneck—effectively blocking INA from being used where it mattered most. **Directive:** Retain the topological awareness and cross-Pod penalty, but reweight the score to prioritize switches that lie on paths to high-volume, cross-Pod jobs. For assignment, relax the Pod constraint and allow assignment to any INA switch on any valid path (including non-same-Pod), but apply a load-aware cost function that penalizes overloading the PS link and favors switches that can aggregate high-volume jobs. Avoid hard-coding Pod-based filtering; instead, use dynamic load estimation to guide assignment.", "functions_to_update": ["_decide_ina_placement_candidates", "_assign_workers_to_aggregators"]}}
